<div class="service-block reverse">
	<div class="service-text reveal--right reveal">
		<section class="callout">
			<p>
				Dialectical Behavior Therapy (DBT) was initially developed to treat Borderline Personality Disorder, and has 
				since been a strong framework used in treating many mental health challenges, including depression, anxiety, 
				trauma symptoms, suicidal ideation, non-sucidal self-injury, aggressive behaviors, eating disorders, 
				substance use disorders, and more.
			</p>

			<p>
				DBT is a treatment model that focuses on supporting individuals with safely manage emotions, strengthening 
				interpersonal and conflict resolution skills, and minimizing potentially harmful behavior (i.e. self-harm, 
				suicidal behavior, substance use, eating disordered behavior, etc.). 
			</p>
		</section>
	</div>
	<div class="service-header">
		<h3>
			Dialectical Behavior Therapy (explain)
		</h3>
	</div>
</div>

<hr class="service-divider">


<div class="service-block">
	<div class="service-text reveal--left reveal">
		<section class="callout">
			<p>
				A “dialectic” means that two truths that seem incongruent can both exist at the same time. A couple of 
				examples: 
				<ul>
					<li>You can be depressed and still experience moments of joy.</li>
					<li>You can want to be alive, and still have moments where you have thoughts of suicide.</li>
					<li>You can love your parents, and still be upset with them.</li>
				</ul>
			</p>

			<p class="citation">
				Prillinger, K., Goreis, A., Macura, S., Hajek Gross, C., Lozar, A., Fanninger, S., ... & Kothgassner, 
				O. D. (2024). A systematic review and meta-analysis on the efficacy of dialectical behavior therapy 
				variants for the treatment of post-traumatic stress disorder. <i>European Journal of Psychotraumatology, 
				15(1)</i>, 2406662. 
			</p>

			<p class="citation">
				Ciesinski, N. K., Sorgi-Wilson, K. M., Cheung, J. C., Chen, E. Y., & McCloskey, M. S. (2022). The effect 
				of dialectical behavior therapy on anger and aggressive behavior: A systematic review with meta-analysis. 
				<i>Behaviour research and therapy, 154</i>, 104122. 
			</p>
		</section>
	</div>
