<div class="service-block reverse">
	<div class="service-text reveal--right reveal">
		<section class="callout">
			<p>
				Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT) focuses on improving your relationship with your own thoughts and 
				feelings using mindfulness and acceptance-based strategies. ACT functions under the understanding that pain 
				is a normal part of the human experience, and that you can experience pain and still live a fulfilling, 
				meaningful life. While using ACT, there will be a focus on identifying your values, restructuring your 
				relationship with thoughts and feelings, and accepting the human experience. The ultimate goals are to 
				increase cognitive flexibility and to live within your own personal values. 
			</p>

			<p>
				There is evidence that ACT can be successful in treating anxiety, depression, OCD, eating disorders, 
				substance use disorders, supporting folks living with chronic pain, and more.
			</p>

			<p class="citation">
				Gloster, A. T., Walder, N., Levin, M. E., Twohig, M. P., & Karekla, M. (2020). The empirical status of 
				acceptance and commitment therapy: A review of meta-analyses. <i>Journal of contextual behavioral science, 
				18</i>, 181-192. 
			</p>
		</section>
