<h3>
	Collaborative Problem Solving (explain)
</h3>
<section class="callout">
	<p>
		Collaborative Problem Solving is a trauma-informed treatment model that addresses challenges through 
		the understanding that everyone is doing the best that they can with the resources they have. This model 
		views lagging skills (rather than a lack of will) and environmental factors are a primary reason for unwanted 
		behavior. This is a solution-focused, collaborative approach that begins by exploring the reason for 
		challenges, then identifying proactive measures to prevent unwanted behavior. 
	</p>

	<p>
		A primary underlying assumption when utilizing a Collaborative Problem Solving approach is 
		that “Kids do well if they can”, which is an assumption that I bring into every therapeutic 
		relationship, regardless of what primary treatment modality we are using.
	</p>


	<p>
		If you're interested in learning more about Collaborative Problem Solving, I recommend visiting 
		<a href="https://thinkkids.org/">Think:Kids | Official home of Collaborative Problem Solving® (CPS).</a>
	</p>
</section>

